---
layout: default
---
{% if page._styles %}
  <!-- Page/Post style -->
  <style type="text/css">
    {{ page._styles }}
  </style>
{% endif %}

<div class="post{% if page.plate %} has-plate{% endif %}">
  {% comment %}
    An optional painting at the head of the page, set above the title so the
    image reads as the plate and the title as its caption line. Declared in
    front matter as `plate:` with path/alt and optional caption/wide.
  {% endcomment %}
  {% if page.plate %}
    <div class="page-plate{% if page.plate.wide %} page-plate--wide{% endif %}">
      {% if page.plate.wide %}
        {% assign plate_sizes = '(min-width: 660px) 515px, (min-width: 576px) 78vw, 92vw' %}
      {% else %}
        {% assign plate_sizes = '(min-width: 660px) 317px, (min-width: 576px) 48vw, 72vw' %}
      {% endif %}
      {%
        include figure.liquid loading="eager" path=page.plate.path class="img-fluid"
        sizes=plate_sizes alt=page.plate.alt caption=page.plate.caption
      %}
    </div>
  {% endif %}

  <header class="post-header">
    <h1 class="post-title">{{ page.title }}</h1>
    {% comment %}
      A plated page's masthead is the painting, the title and the rule — the
      description would be a third line of chrome before any content. It stays
      in front matter because head.liquid still uses it for the meta tag.
    {% endcomment %}
    {% unless page.plate %}
      <p class="post-description">{{ page.description }}</p>
    {% endunless %}
  </header>

  <article>
    {{ content }}
  </article>

  {% if page.related_publications %}
    <h2>References</h2>
    <div class="publications">
      {% bibliography --cited_in_order %}
    </div>
  {% endif %}

  {% if site.giscus and page.giscus_comments %}
    {% include giscus.liquid %}
  {% endif %}
</div>
